treats negative values as smaller than positive, but our loop over unsigned c=0..255 gives 0 as smallest, then 1... up to 127, then 128-255 (which correspond to negative signed chars). Actually in C, comparing `freq[c] > best_count` with c increasing from 0 to 255 means we consider character values 0..255. The original qsort uses signed char comparison: -1 is less than any non-negative because as int it's -1 < 65. So the smallest signed value wins. In our loop, if we iterate c=0..255 and update on > only, then for a tie between label 'A' (c=65) and sentinel (c=255), since 65 comes first, best_label='A'. That's wrong.\n\nWe need to replicate the original tie-breaking: among labels with max frequency, choose the one that is smallest according to signed char ordering. Since we are counting frequencies in an array indexed by unsigned char value, we can after finding max count, scan for the smallest signed char value that has that count. But easier: iterate c from 0..255 but treat them as signed? Actually if we want smallest signed char, negative values (128-255) come before non-negative (0-127). So we should iterate in order of signed char: -128 to 127. That is c=128..255 then 0..127. But original qsort sorts by `*(char*)a - *(char*)b` which uses signed char promotion; so negative values sort first. So the smallest signed value wins.\n\nThus we should find max count, then among all c with freq[c]==max_count, pick the one with smallest signed char value. We can do:\n\n```c\nint best_count = 0;\nfor (int c=0; c<256; ++c) if (freq[c] > best_count) best_count = freq[c];\nchar best_label = 0;\n// find first c in signed order that has max count\nfor (int s=-128; s<=127; ++s) {\n    unsigned char u = (unsigned char)s;\n    if (freq[u] == best_count) { best_label = (char)s; break; }\n}\n```\n\nBut note: original initializes most_frequent to labels[0] and count=1, then scans. If all frequencies are 0? Not possible because k>=1. But we can handle.\n\nAlternatively, we could sort 